#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162864} :activity/id #uuid "33210971-70cd-50fd-974b-053eab8e1cab" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048149 :topic/id #uuid "82e1668e-936b-52f4-a230-dc0cb3dc1f13" :topic/math-academy-id 3133} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162864 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163239 :tutorial/content "We can compute a singular value decomposition (SVD) of any matrix! The algorithm for computing an SVD of a rectangular $m\\times n$ matrix $(m\\neq n)$ is similar to the algorithm for square matrices.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$A = [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a ${\\color{blue}{3}}\\times {\\color{red}{2}}$ matrix. So, to compute the SVD of $A,$ we must write\n\n$$A=U \\Sigma V^T,$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\Sigma$ is a ${\\color{blue}{3}}\\times {\\color{red}{2}}$ matrix with the singular values of $A$ (ordered from largest to smallest) on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n- $U$ is an orthogonal ${\\color{blue}{3}} \\times {\\color{blue}{3}}$ matrix,\n- $V$ is an orthogonal ${\\color{red}{2}} \\times {\\color{red}{2}}$ matrix.\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$ $$\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}$$\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values of $A.$ Computing the eigenvalues of $A^TA,$ we get $$\\lambda_1 = 4, \\qquad \\lambda_2 = 2.$$ Therefore, the singular values of $A$ are $$\\sigma_1 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_1} = 2, \\qquad \\sigma_2 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_2} = \\sqrt{2}.$$\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A^T A$). Seeking non-zero solutions of $(A^T \\! A - \\lambda_1 I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ and $(A^T \\! A - \\lambda_2 I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the unit eigenvectors $$\\mathbf{v}_1 = [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}], \\qquad \\mathbf{v}_2 = [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].$$\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A.$ Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the first two columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\mathbf u_1\\mathbin{:}$ $$\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}$$\n- Then, we compute $\\mathbf u_2\\mathbin{:}$ $$\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}$$\n\n- The matrix $U$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix. However, we do not have the third singular value, so we can't use the same formula to find the third column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector $$\\mathbf{u}_3 =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$$ such that $${\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0,\\end{aligned} \\qquad \\|\\mathbf{u}_3\\|=1.$$ Solving this system, we get the following nonzero normalized solution:\n$$\\begin{aligned}{u}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U,$ $\\Sigma,$ and $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$U = [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] , \\qquad \\Sigma = [\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}], \\qquad V = [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "670f2f0c-5dc5-54ae-9a39-2166ee2414fd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4893 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0ede58be-b8d2-5bb7-a17c-e74da723f67c" :step/math-academy-id 648291 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162951}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162951 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162047 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161841 :question/id #uuid "16e5cc04-aa74-55f7-992b-b9992a8dd1c7" :question/math-academy-id "e-8632"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44c68619-9cf3-5dd8-b3db-00c9730d3308" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050732}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371414} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371421} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388819}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Third Factor in the SVD of a Rectangular Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "93a722c1-3bdf-59db-908f-c5d0b209511b" :step/math-academy-id 648292 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162953}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162952 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162048 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161842 :question/id #uuid "bba077a6-56c6-5137-b949-26fae1c98625" :question/math-academy-id "e-8633"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6732620-fad1-5dd3-98c5-07ba59d0b286" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050776}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370660} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370703} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408052} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408054}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Factor in the SVD of a Rectangular Matrix: Advanced Cases"} :step/id #uuid "2a223abd-811a-561b-aba4-561597ec202e" :step/math-academy-id 648298 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162957}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162953 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162049 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161843 :question/id #uuid "4fd6d412-d080-5fd1-9318-65a0f5f02dc6" :question/math-academy-id "e-8634"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b36c482-75a0-5413-8e3e-4a72e3ddb26a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049957}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162731} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371428} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408053}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Factor in the SVD of a Rectangular Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "1b7070be-3dac-5359-9175-8300793c3568" :step/math-academy-id 648295 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385976}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162954 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163264 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find an SVD for the $2\\times 4$ matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$A = [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].$$\n\nDirectly applying the standard approach to $A$ could be problematic because it requires finding the eigenvalues of the $4\\times 4$ matrix $A^T \\! A.$\n\nIn such cases, it's usually more convenient to find an SVD of the transposed matrix $A^T$ and then take the transpose of the resulting SVD. In this case, we would only have to find the eigenvalues of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFor example, if\n\n$$A^T = U \\Sigma V^T$$\n\nis an SVD for $A^T,$ then\n\n$$A= \\left(A^T\\right)^T= \\left(U \\Sigma V^T\\right)^T=V\\Sigma^T U^T$$\n\nis an SVD for $A.$\n\nGoing through our usual process, we find that an SVD for $A^T$ is\n\n$$A^T= \\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{\\large U} \\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{\\large \\Sigma} \\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]^T}_{\\large V^T}.$$\n\nTaking the transpose of the above equation. we find that an SVD for $A$ is\n\n$$A= V\\Sigma^T U^T = {[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]} {[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]} [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]^T.$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "71ba93f6-01a1-54c4-bda4-b51b9b8dad77" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5110 :tutorial/title "The Singular Value Decomposition of a Transposed Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "e87c19a4-7e24-5962-880c-39d60fa71758" :step/math-academy-id 648304} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162957 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162051 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161845 :question/id #uuid "1b9d8b05-cdb6-555b-b68c-b46000bb6650" :question/math-academy-id "e-8798"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "566bda0f-f62d-581d-936d-baa8ae474e50" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050584}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162733} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370746} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370799}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Factor in the SVD of a Rectangular Matrix: Cases Requiring Orthogonalization"} :step/id #uuid "7cf890cb-057f-5300-9a99-426e3fb959db" :step/math-academy-id 648301 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162954}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385976 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385977 :tutorial/content "In some singular value decomposition problems, we do not get all of the needed vectors from the usual formulas right away.\n\nThis happens, for example, when:\n\n- an eigenvalue is repeated, or\n- a singular value is zero, so the corresponding vector is not determined uniquely.\n\nIn these cases, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Solve the system that defines the eigenspace (or orthogonal complement).\n\nFor example, we solve an equation of the form $$(A^T A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0},$$ or, when finding missing columns of $U,$ we solve orthogonality conditions such as $$\\mathbf{u}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = 0, \\qquad \\mathbf{u}_2 \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = 0.$$\n- **Step 2.** Write the general solution using free variables.\n\nThis shows that there are infinitely many solutions. For example, the solution might look like $$\\mathbf{v} = x\\,\\mathbf{b}_1 + y\\,\\mathbf{b}_2,$$ where $x$ and $y$ are free variables.\n- **Step 3.** Choose convenient vectors from the solution space.\n\nWe usually choose simple values for the free variables to get easy vectors. These vectors should be linearly independent and, if possible, already orthogonal.\n- **Step 4.** Orthogonalize the chosen vectors if necessary.\n\nIf the vectors are not mutually orthogonal, we apply the Gram–Schmidt process.\n- **Step 5.** Normalize the vectors.\n\nAfter that, we divide each vector by its norm to obtain unit vectors.\n\nThese unit vectors form an orthonormal basis for the solution space, and they can be used as the needed columns of $V$ or $U.$\n\n**Note:** In these situations, there is usually *more than one correct answer*. Different choices of free variables can produce different orthonormal bases, so the resulting SVD is not necessarily unique. What matters is that the chosen vectors:\n\n- belong to the correct solution space,\n- are mutually orthogonal, and\n- have norm $1.$\n\nLet's see a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "5e93068a-566a-5c34-a459-3250d8a847d3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 15621 :tutorial/title "Choosing Eigenvectors When Eigenvalues Repeat or Are Missing"} :step/id #uuid "7dc02cbe-4a5d-5f2d-8ad3-baa5e8278916" :step/math-academy-id 648305 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162952}}] :activity/title "Singular Value Decomposition of Larger Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162233}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046679} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408056} :activity/id #uuid "069cd187-e43e-5701-8359-21c488d6fdf7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048149 :topic/id #uuid "82e1668e-936b-52f4-a230-dc0cb3dc1f13" :topic/math-academy-id 3133} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408055 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408052 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1624144f-dc91-5236-84ee-18b96e8277af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407967} {:answer/id #uuid "1f0a89b3-42e1-5c0c-9f1d-976b0b80e3f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "-{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407972} {:answer/id #uuid "746511ed-c96a-5c0b-a954-73fab4f89071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "{x}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407995}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407967} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ef2a40d-dd8f-5054-9101-9b4095ee0f14" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408037} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e47d3ea-4750-55d7-9ad9-f144dbee61aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407976} {:answer/id #uuid "9c719bc8-cc87-56ef-930a-6bf23b501c80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408008} {:answer/id #uuid "cea590ba-fbf3-55d0-a265-d70d43b616